selftext:
In *Rebel Sector*, Cyberblades are support ships that follow your fighter. At full power, they add to your firepower; you can also send them ahead to damage enemies or spend one to clear enemy bullets.



# Collecting and powering up

Upgrade enemies and bosses can drop a Cyberblade pickup when the player is at maximum level and has room for another ship. Collecting one adds a Cyberblade, up to a limit of six. If you collect one while already at the limit, it converts to 1,000 points.

The code currently uses “Option” internally; these constants set the capacity and hold threshold:

    private static final int PLAYER_OPTION_LIMIT = 6;
    static final int PLAYER_MAX_LEVEL = 2;
    private static final float OPTION_HOLD_ACTIVATION_SECONDS = 0.25f;

At maximum level, Cyberblades animate and fire alongside the player. Below maximum level, they still follow, but draw as an inactive sword and don’t fire.  


# Following the player

Each Cyberblade gets a different phase offset based on its place in the list. A pair of sine waves gives the group a loose, shifting formation around the player:

    public static Position calculatePlayerOptionPosition(
        float clock, float offset, float playerX, float playerY
    ) {
        float x = 40 * MathUtils.sin(clock / 60f)
            * MathUtils.sin(.01f * clock + offset) + playerX;
        float y = 40 * MathUtils.cos(.01f * clock + offset) + playerY;
        return new Position(x, y);
    }

# Spending a Cyberblade

A quick press and release of **BOMB** activates a bomb. It consumes the most recently collected Cyberblade and clears enemy bullets. The bomb’s visual effect lasts 60 frames; it doesn’t damage enemies.

    Entity option = game.getPlayerOptionList().remove(
        game.getPlayerOptionList().size() - 1
    );
    game.freeEntity(option);
    freeAll(game.getEnemyBulletList());

So the action is a tradeoff: keep your support fire, deploy the Cyberblades for contact damage, or sacrifice one when you need the screen clear. Under the hood, the Java implementation still uses the internal `Option` naming: Cyberblades are regular `Entity` instances with a few extra state flags, and a separate render system draws them.  


